## How it all connects

```
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │        PUBLIC INTERNET       │
                    │   globally routable IPs      │
                    └───────────────┬───────────────┘
                                    │
                              resolved by
                                    │
                                    ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │             DNS               │
                    │     names  →  IP addresses    │
                    └───────────────┬───────────────┘
                                    │
                          translated at boundary
                                    │
                                    ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │        NAT GATEWAY            │◄──── VPN tunnel
                    │  private IP  ↔  public IP     │      (alternate way in,
                    └───────────────┬───────────────┘       bypasses NAT boundary)
                                    │
                                    ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │     SUBNETTING / VLANs        │
                    │   segments the private LAN    │
                    └───────────────┬───────────────┘
                                    │
                                    ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │            DHCP               │
                    │      auto-assigns IPs         │
                    └───────────────┬───────────────┘
                                    │
                                    ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────────┐
                    │             HOST              │
                    │  e.g. 192.168.1.10            │
                    │  ports: 22, 80, 443 ...       │
                    └─────────────────────────────┘
```

Reading it top to bottom: a request from the public internet is resolved by DNS, translated at the NAT boundary into the private network, then carved into segments by subnetting/VLANs. Inside a segment, DHCP assigns hosts their addresses, and each host exposes services on specific ports. A VPN sits alongside NAT as an alternate way in: it tunnels a remote device past the NAT boundary so it behaves as if it's local to the segmented private network.

---

## Mindset

Every one of these exists to solve a scaling problem for the same two questions: how do devices reach each other, and how does data know which device (and which service) it's meant for? If you forget a specific detail, you should be able to re-derive why the concept exists just from those two questions. That's the difference between memorizing terms and understanding the stack.